1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERA: Turing took it on, he said, because no one else was doing anything about it, and he could have it to himself.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERB: It also took captured papers: in May 1941, codebooks and keys from U one-ten.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERA: Through late nineteen forty-one, decrypts helped steer convoys around the waiting U-boats.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERB: Then, on the 1st of February 1942, the U-boats got a new Enigma with four rotors.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERA: For ten months, Bletchley couldn't read them, and the wolf packs ravaged convoys.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERB: So how did they get back in?

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: In October nineteen forty-two, off Port Said, sailors from H M S Petard climbed into the sinking U five-five-nine for its papers.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERB: Two of them, Tony Fasson and Colin Grazier, were still inside when it sank, and drowned.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERA: By December nineteen forty-two, U-boat messages were being read again.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERB: So how much did that decide the Atlantic?

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERA: That's still argued. Radar and much else mattered too.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERB: In the film The Imitation Game, Turing builds one machine almost alone, and calls it Christopher.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERA: In fact, the first bombe was Victory, and breaking Enigma took thousands of people.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERB: Thousands?

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: By January nineteen forty-five, almost nine thousand people worked at Bletchley Park and its outstations. About three quarters were women.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERB: And what was all that work worth?

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: Harry Hinsley, a veteran and later Bletchley's official historian, judged their work shortened the war by at least two years.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERB: Others find any such number too precise. No one can run a war twice.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERA: One of the last messages Bletchley decoded was sent from Cuxhaven, on the seventh of May, nineteen forty-five. British troops had entered the town.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERB: And the operator signed off. Closing down forever. All the best. Goodbye.
